{
	title: 'Every terminal scrollback clear has a recorded reason',
	spec: 'Invariant: the terminal client clears scrollback (CSI 3J) only through one function that requires a reason. A reason names the cause and its evidence: a forced repaint and its trigger (Ctrl-L or /redraw, resize, resume, tab switch, the first paint with two or more tabs, the top out of reach after a shrink of the terminal); a frame shrink (old and new row counts, the first changed row, and the blocks whose rows shrank, with their block ids such as #t239 and what changed, e.g. tool output collapsed, card closed); or a change to a row already in scrollback (the row, the writable top, the block id and what changed). Snap logging is a common boolean setting, snapLog, in config.ason (src/common/settings.ts), off by default; nothing is recorded or written while it is off. When on, the client appends one record per clear to a bounded, owner-only (0600) file in the client home state directory, rejecting symlinks, and keeps the newest 500. Each record holds the local time, the session the tab shows, the reason fields and one plain-English sentence explaining the snap, so a user can ask a session in the Hal home "why did that snap" and the model answers from the file. Records hold no transcript text, tool arguments or output, typed keys, URLs, credentials, hostnames or paths. The /snaps client command shows the newest records with their sentences, or says that snapLog is off and how to turn it on. Behavioral tests: off records nothing; each clear path records its reason; a shrink names the shrinking block.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['4k'],
	foldInto: ['4k', 'cc']
}
